import{_ as a,o as t,c as n,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.hw3Av5Vn.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Installing","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"INSTALLING.md","filePath":"INSTALLING.md"}'),i={name:"INSTALLING.md"};function o(l,e,r,h,d,p){return t(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s(`<h1 id="installing" tabindex="-1">Installing <a class="header-anchor" href="#installing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Installing&quot;">​</a></h1><p>llamatron runs in a terminal on a Mac, and on Linux for everything but FluidSynth&#39;s installer. This page covers installing llamatron, and two optional components: FluidSynth, which plays every demo with no synthesizer, and a faster YAML parser. When it&#39;s installed, <a href="./GETTING_STARTED">Getting Started</a> is the next page.</p><h2 id="requirements" tabindex="-1">Requirements <a class="header-anchor" href="#requirements" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Requirements&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Python 3.15</li><li>git</li><li>A terminal at least 80 columns wide and 24 rows tall. llamatron draws with bold, dim, reverse video and box characters, so it suits any colour scheme.</li></ul><h2 id="installation" tabindex="-1">Installation <a class="header-anchor" href="#installation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Installation&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Clone the repository and run the setup script from the project folder:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>git clone https://github.com/mpdehaan/llamatron.git</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>cd llamatron</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>sh user_scripts/setup.sh</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The script makes a Python virtual environment in the folder <code>.venv</code> and installs llamatron into it, together with its dependencies, <a href="https://github.com/mido/mido" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">mido</a>, python-rtmidi, PyYAML and <a href="https://textual.textualize.io/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Textual</a>, and the Live set that exports are built from. llamatron is installed in editable form, so the command always runs the code in the folder you cloned, and a <code>git pull</code> updates it without installing again.</p><p>The <code>llamatron</code> command lives in the virtual environment, so typing <code>llamatron</code> works once the environment is activated with <code>source .venv/bin/activate</code>, which each new terminal needs once. From the project folder, <code>./llamatron.sh</code> runs llamatron without activating anything, and every other script in <code>user_scripts/</code> and <code>internal_tools/</code> finds the environment by itself.</p><h2 id="fluidsynth-optional" tabindex="-1">FluidSynth (Optional) <a class="header-anchor" href="#fluidsynth-optional" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;FluidSynth (Optional)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>FluidSynth is a free software synthesizer, and it is the quickest way to hear llamatron: with it, every demo plays as it comes, with no synthesizer, no DAW and no MIDI setup, and a song can be exported as a WAV file. Install it now if you want to hear the demos right away, or if you have no synthesizer or DAW set up; skip it if you will play everything through your own instruments. On a Mac, from the project folder:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>sh user_scripts/install_fluidsynth.sh</span></span></code></pre></div><p><a href="./FLUIDSYNTH">FluidSynth</a> describes what the script installs, how the tracker uses FluidSynth, and when to use your own instruments instead. To check that it works, open a demo and play it:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>./llamatron.sh examples/learning/13_classic_strum.yml</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Press <code>A</code> for Arrange, move to the top left corner with the arrows, where <code>▶ song</code> shows, and press <code>Space</code>.</p><h2 id="faster-yaml-parser-optional" tabindex="-1">Faster YAML Parser (Optional) <a class="header-anchor" href="#faster-yaml-parser-optional" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Faster YAML Parser (Optional)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Set files are YAML, and PyYAML reads them about ten times faster when it&#39;s built with <a href="https://github.com/yaml/libyaml" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">libyaml</a>, which makes a large set open almost at once. Everything works without it. To see which parser you have:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>.venv/bin/python -c &quot;import yaml; print(yaml.__with_libyaml__)&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>True</code> means libyaml is in use. The PyYAML wheels on PyPI include it, so a Python version that has a wheel gets it with the install above. For a Python version with no wheel yet, pip builds PyYAML from source, and includes libyaml only if it finds the library and a working C compiler at that moment, so install those first and then rebuild:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>brew install libyaml                                   # macOS; on Debian or Ubuntu: sudo apt install libyaml-dev</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>.venv/bin/python -m pip install --force-reinstall --no-binary pyyaml --no-cache-dir &quot;pyyaml==6.0.3&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>On a Mac the compiler is Xcode&#39;s, whose license has to have been accepted once (<code>sudo xcodebuild -license accept</code>), and the library has to match the Python: an Apple Silicon Python needs the Apple Silicon Homebrew in <code>/opt/homebrew</code>, not the Intel one in <code>/usr/local</code>. If the C parser can&#39;t be built, pip quietly builds PyYAML without it, and llamatron falls back to the pure-Python parser.</p>`,21)])])}const m=a(i,[["render",o]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
